FatFs in ReactOS
================

This directory contains FatFs R0.16 with the upstream patch 2 applied.
It is the common FAT/exFAT implementation used by the image host tool,
FreeLdr, and the exFAT filesystem driver. Each consumer supplies only the
FatFs media-access and allocation callbacks appropriate to its environment.

Upstream: https://elm-chan.org/fsw/ff/
Archive:  https://elm-chan.org/fsw/ff/arc/ff16.zip
Archive SHA-256: 99f7dc1f7e095356e4a9e3dbe29959090d8b948afe2bbc5441e52fdf4b85449e
Release: R0.16, July 22, 2025

Applied upstream patches, in order:

1. https://elm-chan.org/fsw/ff/patch/ff16p1.diff
   SHA-256: 7996ddc3135f3d534a8153f7d75d587030e5c8551b003c3b7ee823ffb7fc64bf
2. https://elm-chan.org/fsw/ff/patch/ff16p2.diff
   SHA-256: 5cd39f1fc299f0f1dec9fa1ce544dc0bc048b2f6eb3b8161476ed20f9cf1e290

ReactOS changes are intentionally limited to:

* ffconf.h enables exFAT, UTF-8 long names, formatting, 64-bit LBA, and
  the media geometry needed by all three consumers. FF_LFN_UNICODE remains
  overridable so the kernel exFAT driver can use UTF-16 without a second copy
  of the configuration file.
* ff.h accepts FF_USE_REACTOS_TYPES so target builds use the SDK's fixed-width
  Windows types without including the user-mode Windows umbrella header.
* CMakeLists.txt defines the target-side static library.
* ff.c provides ReactOS-only replacements for selected exFAT optimizations
  identified in "Patent-related changes" below.

Patent-related changes
----------------------

The __REACTOS__ branches replace selected upstream NameHash and NoFatChain
optimization bodies. Where upstream contains an implementation, the #else
branch contains only a patent and expiration-date placeholder.
All in-tree consumers define __REACTOS__; ff.c intentionally rejects a build
without it rather than compiling the incomplete placeholder paths.

1. Directory lookup by NameHash: US8321439B2 (adjusted expiration date
   2027-03-09) and US10614032B2 (adjusted expiration date 2028-01-27).
   dir_find() compares candidate name lengths and characters without using
   the on-disk NameHash as a pre-filter.

2. NoFatChain state and flag handling: US8725772B2 (anticipated expiration
   date 2027-05-23). remove_chain(), f_mkdir(), and f_expand() keep locally
   modified chains on the FAT instead of marking them contiguous.

3. NoFatChain extension: US9122695B2 and US9558223B2 (anticipated expiration
   date 2027-05-23), and US7873596B2 (adjusted expiration date 2028-03-27).
   create_chain() records new and extended chains in the FAT.

These dates and statuses were checked in Google Patents on 2026-07-24.
Google's status data is an estimate, not a legal conclusion. Recheck the
USPTO file history before replacing any placeholder.

Reading volumes written elsewhere
---------------------------------

None of the above removes the NoFatChain flag from the on-disk format. A
volume written by another implementation may hold objects whose allocation is
described only by that flag, and for those upstream get_fat() generates the
cluster numbers arithmetically instead of reading the FAT. That path is kept
deliberately: without it such objects cannot be read at all. The line this
port maintains is that the flag is honoured only for format-required access
and is never produced, set, extended, or converted:

* No code assigns the contiguous state. Its only source is the on-disk flag,
  loaded by load_obj_xdir() as (XDIR_GenFlags & 2).
* Objects created here are recorded on the FAT and stored with GenFlags = 1,
  including those from f_expand(), f_mkdir() and f_mkfs().
* Files carrying the flag can be opened for reading but not writing.
* A directory carrying the flag can be traversed, but dir_next() refuses to
  stretch it. create_chain() rejects any missed attempt to stretch or convert
  such a chain before allocating a cluster.
* No code added by this port selects an access path from the flag. The
  f_lseek() batching described below applies only to FAT-recorded chains;
  objects carrying the flag take the generic loop.

The NameHash field is likewise still written on entry creation, because a
volume without it is not readable by other implementations. It is never used
for lookup; f_rename() only preserves the value generated for the new name.

The kernel driver and FreeLoader do not select data I/O paths from exFAT
allocation flags or construct file extents. File data is accessed through
FatFs. The driver's directory index keys on a hash of the path string that
the driver computes itself (ExFatHashPath); it does not read the on-disk
NameHash field.

The driver's additional large-transfer batching operates below FatFs allocation
logic. It uses bounded adaptive read-ahead and write combining for consecutive
sector requests; the batching path does not inspect directory allocation flags
or derive file extents.

Performance changes
-------------------

These local paths are compiled only when __REACTOS__ is defined.

* ff.c f_lseek(): for exFAT objects opened read-only whose chain is recorded
  on the FAT, the seek follows the chain with one sector-window load per FAT
  sector instead of one get_fat() call per cluster. The walk
  tracks the first entry of the loaded sector, so the inner loop indexes the
  window and tests the sector boundary without the runtime divisions by the
  sector size that get_fat() performs per call. The loads, value checks, and
  error paths are identical to the generic loop; no chain information is
  retained across calls.
* ff.h/ff.c: exFAT builds keep a FAT linearity digest — two bits per FAT
  sector recording whether every in-range entry e of that sector holds
  exactly e + 1. It is indexed by FAT sector and describes the content of
  the allocation table itself; it stores no file identity, no logical
  offset, and no per-object run, so it is not a file allocation map. It is
  filled from sectors that are read anyway, discarded for a sector whenever
  put_fat() modifies it, and released at unmount, remount, or format.
  get_fat() and the f_lseek chain walk answer from it without loading the
  sector, so following a chain through a linear region costs neither a
  sector load nor a per-cluster step. Values returned are identical to
  reading the FAT.
* ff.h/ff.c: exFAT write builds give the allocation bitmap its own sector
  window (bwin/bwinsect/bwflag with move_bitmap_window/sync_bitmap_window).
  find_bitmap(), change_bitmap(), and the f_getfree() bitmap scan use it
  exclusively, so per-cluster allocation no longer evicts the FAT sector
  from fs->win between every FAT and bitmap update. A dirty bitmap window
  is written before dependent FAT or directory updates, while removal
  flushes the FAT update before clearing its allocation bits.

The media-access implementation is not kept in this directory. This preserves
the upstream separation between the filesystem core and platform I/O.
